cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this teaching material, or the models in it, please cite it as below."
title: "Advanced Optimization Modeling: a lithium supply-chain series"
abstract: >-
  Fifteen executed teaching notebooks and an accompanying Python package covering
  deterministic and stochastic supply-chain optimization on a lithium battery
  chain: MILP network design, two-stage stochastic programming, Benders and
  progressive hedging, CVaR and minimax risk measures, Wright's-law learning
  curves, Cournot and Stackelberg games, MPEC, network interdiction, and a
  closed-loop recycling model. Each notebook builds its model by hand and then
  asserts agreement with the packaged implementation to within 1e-9.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Jones
    given-names: Erick
    name-suffix: Jr.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0559-4699"
    affiliation: "University of Texas at Arlington"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sear-labs/advopt-lithiumsc"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - optimization
  - supply chain
  - lithium
  - stochastic programming
  - teaching material
  - operations research
# No doi: yet, and that is deliberate rather than an oversight -- a DOI cannot
# exist before the first release. The order is forced:
#   1. this file, with no doi                         <- you are here
#   2. connect the repo in Zenodo, tag a release
#   3. Zenodo mints a VERSION doi and a CONCEPT doi
#   4. add the CONCEPT doi here and commit
# The concept DOI always resolves to the latest version and never changes, so
# step 4 is a one-time edit and not a per-release chore. See CLAUDE.md Part 5,
# "Every published repo carries a licence AND a citation".
